threshold_ecdsa — provenance note
==================================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `frost`/
`adaptor`/`bls12_381` modules — self-contained crypto modules not folded
into the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

threshold_ecdsa — Phase 2a (trusted-dealer keygen) of a GG20 threshold-ECDSA
implementation, clean-room from two public IACR ePrint papers:

  - R. Gennaro, S. Goldfeder, "One Round Threshold ECDSA with Identifiable
    Abort", IACR ePrint 2020/540 ("GG20") — the overall protocol this arc
    (2a keygen / 2b MtA+range-proofs / 2c signing) targets.
  - R. Gennaro, S. Goldfeder, "Fast Multiparty Threshold ECDSA with Fast
    Trustless Setup", IACR ePrint 2019/114 ("GG18") — the ring-Pedersen
    auxiliary-parameter construction `AuxParams`/`generateAuxParams`
    references (GG18 §4.1's "aux info" generation, which GG20 also relies
    on for its own range proofs), AND (Phase 2c, `zkproofs.zig`) GG18
    Appendix A's zero-knowledge range-proof construction ("Alice's range
    proof" Πᵢ and Bob's MtA proof Π^MtA/Π^MtAwc) that this module's
    `RangeProof`/`MtaProof`/`MtaProofWc` scaffold and `proveAliceRange`/
    `verifyAliceRange`/`proveBobMta`/`verifyBobMta`/`proveBobMtaWc`/
    `verifyBobMtaWc` doc comments target.

**Phase 2d (`signing.zig`) provenance.** The online signing protocol
(`signWithShares` and its round-by-round construction: `k_i`/`γ_i`/`w_i`
sampling, the Γ commit-reveal, the pairwise checked-MtA/MtAwc invocation
pattern, `δ`/`R`/`r` derivation, `s_i`/`s` computation, low-S
normalization, and the fail-closed final self-check) was implemented
against GG20 (ePrint 2020/540)'s overall signing-protocol SHAPE (the
MtA-driven `k·γ`/`k·x` share-conversion structure GG18 §4 and GG20 both
document) — no specific paper section's exact round numbering or message
formats were transcribed; this is this module's own composition of its
ALREADY-implemented Phase 2b/2c primitives (`mta`/`zkproofs`), not a
port of any external signing-round implementation. The Γ commit-reveal's
knowledge-of-exponent proof (`signing.SchnorrProof`) is the well-known
textbook non-interactive Schnorr Sigma protocol for discrete-log knowledge
— common cryptographic folklore, not itself copyrightable, and NOT
transcribed from GG20's own `Πzk` formalization (that section of the paper
was not consulted for this pass — see `signing.zig`'s module doc comment
and `SPEC.md`'s Phase 2d section for the resulting "not verified
byte-for-byte against the paper's exact instantiation" caveat). GG20 §4's
actual identifiable-abort culprit-naming apparatus was deliberately NOT
implemented (left `@panic`-stubbed as `signing.identifyAbortCulprit`,
referencing the paper section by name only — no attempt was made to guess
its construction without reading it). The FINAL verification target,
`std.crypto.sign.ecdsa.EcdsaSecp256k1Sha256`, is Zig's own standard-library
ECDSA implementation, used via its public API only.

Both are public specifications/papers, not copyrightable works (merger
doctrine — see `CONVENTIONS.md` §5) — no NOTICE entry is strictly required
for citing them, but is included here for provenance clarity given the
module's overall crypto-protocol nature (matching this repo's `frost`/
`adaptor` precedent of a module-local NOTICE for self-contained crypto
modules). No third-party SOURCE was ported or consulted; `generateAuxParams`'s
doc comment additionally references, BY NAME ONLY (facts about the widely-
used construction, not a source consulted), the "aux info" generation shape
commonly cited by tss-lib- and multi-party-ecdsa-style reference
implementations and by Canetti, Gennaro, Goldfeder, Makriyannis, Peled, "UC
Non-Interactive, Proactive, Threshold ECDSA" §3.1 — no source from any of
these was read or copied; this scaffolding pass worked from the GG18/GG20
papers' own text.

**Phase 2c (`zkproofs.zig`) provenance.** The Sigma-protocol prove/verify
number theory (GG18 Appendix A.1 "Range Proof", A.2 "Respondent ZK Proof
for MtAwc", A.3 "Respondent ZK Proof for MtA") was implemented against the
ACTUAL GG18 paper text — retrieved during the implementation pass from the
NSF Public Access mirror (par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10085440) of the CCS'18
version, which carries the same Appendix A as ePrint 2019/114 — and
structurally cross-checked against bnb-chain/tss-lib's `crypto/mta`
(`range_proof.go`, `proofs.go`, `share_protocol.go`) for the exact sampling
ranges and verifier bounds. The tss-lib cross-check was FACTS/STRUCTURE ONLY
(which value is sampled from which range; which bound the verifier checks) —
NO tss-lib source code was ported or transcribed; that project is
MIT-licensed and is recorded here purely as a structure/bounds reference.
Two verifier bounds (`t1 <= q^7` and the correspondingly wider `gamma`/`tau`
sampling for Bob's `beta'` mask) follow tss-lib/GG20 practice and are
STRICTLY STRONGER than the GG18 paper's own A.2/A.3 (which leave `beta'`
unbounded) — documented as such in `zkproofs.zig`'s module doc comment and
SPEC.md. The Fiat-Shamir transcript (SHA-256, domain-separated) is this
module's own and is NOT byte-compatible with tss-lib's (SHA512/256), so no
reference KAT exists; the tests are self-consistency + the reject contract.
See SPEC.md's "verification level" note (external cryptographic review
recommended before production use).

The Shamir-secret-sharing + Feldman-VSS + Lagrange-interpolation core
(`splitSecretKey`, `groupPublicKey`, `derivePublicKeyShare`,
`reconstructSecret`) is a direct, field/group-swapped PORT of this
repo's OWN prior work — `frost.deriveInterpolatingValue` (RFC 9591 Appendix
C, see `modules/frost/NOTICE`) and `bls12_381.threshold`'s
`evalPolynomialAt`/`feldmanCommitCoefficient`/`derivePublicKeyShare`/
`combineSignatures` (see `modules/bls12_381/NOTICE`) — same authorship,
ported not copied from a third party. No new third-party source or design
reference was introduced for this part.

`std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1` (curve group, `basePoint`/`identityElement`,
`add`/`mul`, `fromSec1`/`toCompressedSec1`, `rejectIdentity`) and
`std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1.scalar.Scalar` (the scalar field Zq,
`fromBytes`/`fromBytes48`/`toBytes`/`add`/`sub`/`mul`/`invert`/`random`) are
used via their public APIs — the same primitives `frost`/`bip340`/`musig2`
already build on in this repo. `std.crypto.ff.Modulus`/`.Fe`
(`AuxModulus`/`AuxFe`) is the same fixed-width constant-time modular-
arithmetic primitive `rsa`/`paillier` build on. No new curve, field, or hash
primitive is implemented here.

The sibling `paillier` module (see `modules/paillier/README.md` and its
entry in the root `NOTICE`) is a build dependency: `keygenTrustedDealer`
wires each party's `paillier.KeyPair` (from `paillier.generate` or
`paillier.fromPrimes`) into its `KeyShare` — a thin, judgment-free
composition, no new Paillier-specific crypto judgment introduced in this
module.

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.

**Status: keygen + aux-params + MtA + Phase-2c range proofs + Phase-2d
online signing all implemented.** The Shamir/Feldman/Lagrange keygen core,
the Paillier-keygen wiring, the ring-Pedersen `generateAuxParams`
safe-prime number theory, and the semi-honest MtA share conversion
(`mta.zig`) are all real and self-consistency-tested. Phase 2c
(`zkproofs.zig` + `mta.zig`'s `mtaAliceInitChecked`/`mtaBobResponseChecked`/
`mtaAliceFinalizeChecked`) — the GG18 Appendix A zero-knowledge MtA range
proofs (STRUCTS, byte codecs, Fiat-Shamir transcript, the six prove/verify
functions, and the fail-closed checked-MtA control flow) — is real and
tested (verified against the paper; see the Phase-2c provenance note
above). Phase 2d (`signing.zig`) composes 2b/2c into the full online
signing protocol; its decisive test chains trusted-dealer keygen through
signing to a real `std.crypto.sign.ecdsa.EcdsaSecp256k1Sha256` verification
and PASSES. The one deliberate Phase-2d scope cut — GG20's identifiable-
abort culprit-naming apparatus, `@panic`-stubbed as
`signing.identifyAbortCulprit` — is documented in `SPEC.md`'s Phase-2d
section and does not affect the "never return an invalid signature"
invariant. External cryptographic review of `zkproofs.zig` (and, by
extension, `signing.zig`, which is built entirely on it) against GG18
Appendix A is recommended before production signing use.
